#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125538} :activity/id #uuid "c3fad3be-1ce2-5167-98d1-4498a125e7e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125839 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is called **diagonalizable** if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSo, to **diagonalize** a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ that satisfy the equation above.\n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/9d/9dee6d9184932a79f36305b9fca2bbb5a2a18bc79b49ad97038f780a2f125e49.svg)\n\nTo diagonalize a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, the eigenvalues are $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3.$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get \n\n![](images/ca/caa7716ce45eef38d92092d27827d30fd539fca27067c1e12f485a719b4f37ae.svg)\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3,$ respectively.\n\nNote that $\\left\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. This becomes important later.\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P.$\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the diagonal. For now, we can write them in any order. So, we let\n\n![](images/3e/3e0df792d822773528cb07cceb39fcb98b7397ae70ab4f114e1450f1d91256d1.svg)\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We need to write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D.$ So, we get\n\n![](images/86/869a05b6273dbbb1b53d9f842d8b2c612017517a0b007c1c5f494f55439a11b4.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=PD{P}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check our solution by computing the product on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f56a4e0-1c9c-573c-95a9-92a93682f0a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0dec286b-a54f-500f-b0be-886c4e33f4ba" :step/math-academy-id 31836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125840 :tutorial/content "There is a theorem that states the following:\n\n*If $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, then it is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A.$*\n\nWe can break this down into two possible cases:\n\n- If $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues, then it is indeed diagonalizable. This is because two distinct eigenvalues will give rise to two linearly independent eigenvectors (one for each eigenvalue). \n\nSo, for a matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ and corresponding eigenvectors $\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2},$ we have\n![](images/e1/e1468bc5ccdff43e33587c7b4b500bc527ab0ac7e74410ea53df1f803a19b520.svg)\n\n- If we get a double root $\\lambda$ of the characteristic equation, the matrix will be diagonalizable *only if* we can find exactly two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda,$ which may or may not be possible. Another way of saying this is that the corresponding eigenspace $V_\\lambda$ must be of dimension $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fbab2132-0247-5376-a13b-df70908230d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5084 :tutorial/title "Diagonalizable vs. Non-Diagonalizable Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "620e7d54-99b9-517c-83f8-d408a4768c65" :step/math-academy-id 31837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124599 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124411 :question/id #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455441}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"} :step/id #uuid "62cef711-c61d-5145-9dcc-a1e378b6208e" :step/math-academy-id 31838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125541}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125841 :tutorial/content "If $A$ is a diagonalizable matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can write this equation in an equivalent form, making $D$ the subject.\n\nFirst, we left-multiply both sides of the equation by $P^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =PD{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}\\cdot A & ={P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}A & ={I}_{2}D{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}A & =D{P}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we right-multiply both sides of the equation by $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1}A\\cdot P & =D{P}^{-1}\\cdot P \\\\ {P}^{-1}AP & =D{I}_{2} \\\\ {P}^{-1}AP & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nD = P^{-1}A P.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e0a81c7-a393-5e5d-b8c1-16d982404f1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5085 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between a Matrix and Its Diagonal Form"} :step/id #uuid "0692f7d4-6f83-540e-bf5f-78b4908292bd" :step/math-academy-id 31841 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124600 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124412 :question/id #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8573"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399124}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "232d082f-7199-5eb9-b914-190d04e57153" :step/math-academy-id 31842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125543}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124601 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124413 :question/id #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404114}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "09beebc7-7088-5b66-8674-578c829c0cb9" :step/math-academy-id 31845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125545}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124602 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124414 :question/id #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" :question/math-academy-id "e-8575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455503}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "552c7c49-4812-5279-9818-9d8769362100" :step/math-academy-id 31848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125842 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ the diagonalization \n\n![](images/76/76843143729d2bc62fc74dc6f03c24eb6a12dfc16a2609a8f2b36499cf39ce90.svg)\n\nis almost unique, but not quite.\n\nWhen we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ we can put the eigenvalues in any order. So this matrix is unique up to permutations of the order of the eigenvalues. Note that by swapping the eigenvalues in $D,$ we then have to swap the corresponding eigenvectors (columns) in $P{:}$\n\n![](images/de/dec5fece3d89eaa022b4e951e63259b0f87213997397b1f8a91fb8fdabed0f74.svg)\n\nAlso, since any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector as well, the eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can consist of these multiples instead. So the invertible matrix $P$ is unique up to multiples of its columns." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb055132-9c7d-55a5-a2c6-0bcb54877c1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5086 :tutorial/title "Uniqueness of the Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "a11b9aec-3c2f-5a01-8b7c-600351ec33e2" :step/math-academy-id 31854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125896 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) be a diagonalizable ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix. Then we can write\n![](images/d8/d8f75220bef8327a8dfc49a6ae22e79c6f1649aefdc8fb43065e0616975a8c5c.svg)\nwhere\n\n- ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) is an invertible matrix whose columns are eigenvectors ![](images/13/13cfebc7f22b3c6cfe7a66492040f9c7682e27e72b0a9adbc033e6a815c68fd9.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0c6e1eed1577bd5d71e38657d494bf53798b9c1f52c54e4a270e200df1cc351.svg) of ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and\n\n- ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are the corresponding eigenvalues ![](images/ff/ff5243fdeda78658220d1bcd4ac9b5963157b5e1f8b6a31231c68a6743248b19.svg) and ![](images/4a/4a2dd44cea07e06ccec03fff120c7fdbcebe19ad7a31d8abde1ab95c8a6b53a1.svg)\n\nTo understand this better, we need to distinguish between two coordinate systems.\n\nIn ![](images/fb/fb3130e489fdca80d1a077702861c20532fe613f9942d48fa1ec24055494a383.svg) the **standard basis** is\n![](images/d2/d29d3ee4ae42a8d4ee5304cb0d29c6c811838d7b893a3a831b1b1b8b3af12561.svg)\n\nSo, if a vector is written as\n![](images/bb/bbbc0379f1691a30dec4c34ec6171db1f116bec12a24891169957af68164f8d3.svg)\nthen ![](images/12/12adb8ba7ed45d89c9d9a80eb7c06616c41999e9fac57732bc24d2b55e1f8073.svg) The numbers ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) and ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg) are the *standard coordinates* of ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nNow let\n![](images/58/58cdfb66e4c93d643fb97273610b3bbf67c8831516d4ccfc5b7790d55a169b28.svg)\n\nSince the columns of ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) are ![](images/13/13cfebc7f22b3c6cfe7a66492040f9c7682e27e72b0a9adbc033e6a815c68fd9.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0c6e1eed1577bd5d71e38657d494bf53798b9c1f52c54e4a270e200df1cc351.svg) written in standard coordinates, the matrix ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) converts coordinates from the basis ![](images/be/be2d796b77bebb31c0c06cef6af82bc9fb606a584a52cc5385cd32c4e143236c.svg) to the standard basis.\n\nSo, for any vector ![](images/b2/b2cb236f7a7f426f303b1391f76900b47f7d2fa1ad9c8110d8584182559017fd.svg)\n![](images/7e/7e5a6a4b9bee9550e68c75a3945ad25d41f1de6dd3b089ce89005428da169f9d.svg)\n\nHere, ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) denotes the standard-coordinate column of the vector, while ![](images/3f/3f7132e53e5a27bee4c84bdddbac07971ec44ce12539c0e0c23bf0006057e199.svg) denotes the coordinate column of the same vector in the eigenvector basis ![](images/8d/8d16f7d70344ddea7a4fe48105e5ea75b984f48fd77f6f6eb89d26ad91b65805.svg)\n\nAlso, in the basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg) the eigenvectors have especially simple coordinate columns:\n![](images/9f/9f5fec87a4253a3a1741c3380e2c6f9331928883ab2083dc02bf0e8efda6a98a.svg)\n\nThis is because\n![](images/30/306d2bb3211dac2883b1eb6abaed54259272a8bcc6a4e562ff5da6fc52dee520.svg)\n\nSo, in the basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg) the matrix ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) acts by scaling the first coordinate by ![](images/ff/ff5243fdeda78658220d1bcd4ac9b5963157b5e1f8b6a31231c68a6743248b19.svg) and the second coordinate by ![](images/4a/4a2dd44cea07e06ccec03fff120c7fdbcebe19ad7a31d8abde1ab95c8a6b53a1.svg)\n\nWe can summarize this process for any vector ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) as a sequence of three steps:\n\n- first, rewrite ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) in the eigenvector basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg)\n\n- then, apply the diagonal matrix ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) in that basis,\n\n- finally, convert the result back to standard coordinates.\n\nThe diagram below shows this sequence.\n\n![](images/14/1459b0643f99c186f9710118617f89f34a601526e7154213471404d2feb2aaeb.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "61524e43-2735-5e5a-904b-2c4973df94a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7958 :tutorial/title "Diagonalization as a Change of Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "831d0bbd-aa17-5012-9d8b-073e0d2d67a6" :step/math-academy-id 45769}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124753}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436966} :activity/id #uuid "6f8bc57b-28dc-577c-84b2-316db148cde5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15560ba1-c0e7-57a3-bd24-dc19e7e9f52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404109} {:answer/id #uuid "4a752dce-688f-5fd3-becb-69a53449004c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404110} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bff80f-44fe-590e-825f-851b2661193c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404111} {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404112} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20c357-a80f-5d68-9f17-47dfa214c55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404112} :answer-field/id #uuid "958185d9-bfb3-5c99-9580-566f3546885e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80493" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "260bb0c0-f4cc-5171-8f8e-251abb929092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20692b0c-ecf3-5857-b371-5e00dd088456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404116} {:answer/id #uuid "8297858c-6a75-5e90-8004-887fd5f4fe6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404117} {:answer/id #uuid "21bdb776-d042-5bc3-8def-4d142fb67170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404118} {:answer/id #uuid "9a56eb51-adbb-529e-b66c-37695fb34a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404119} {:answer/id #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404120} :answer-field/id #uuid "5324a7ce-971d-53cb-b107-19a127a08e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f53e1d4c-a53d-5c3e-8ae8-0e080a8e331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80496" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c086434a-2e61-5437-a45d-1dd3277fb4b0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b5beb30-0a75-5fc1-9136-9135683b1b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346129} {:answer/id #uuid "70a5b71d-efb0-58ab-af42-12eed66f2bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346339} {:answer/id #uuid "783aadac-abc2-5341-8883-bae757b2fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -11\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346406} {:answer/id #uuid "78aeeb6e-6d38-58dc-93e3-4da1e56572c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346408} {:answer/id #uuid "df14a525-a2b3-5a1d-8fb5-695c415e41e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347312} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb4a3c4-e3c2-59e1-b4f8-a58de2ef0e9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9500f77-53af-537c-9030-31932253fda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80372" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7283a38-9ca3-5452-bb36-0cf434eae378" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091ed9c8-701c-5cb1-b4b2-8dafbd6659dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321219} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4e4736-139c-5bc4-aa44-e138e20e88fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321251} {:answer/id #uuid "3e039b3f-0f39-5295-b15a-c8fb9166f65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321634} {:answer/id #uuid "81d4c416-dbd3-50b6-9617-7a656869e7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322169} {:answer/id #uuid "88434e58-c606-599d-b3d0-8af7287c81ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321219} :answer-field/id #uuid "499f430b-3198-504e-8f47-b2cafb6155fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2785be4b-345b-5745-9527-bd2763533b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36929" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."} :step/id #uuid "f50c4f35-60de-5348-bbfd-204a572ac060" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3641611f-7342-59e6-8eb1-e3d5c19f0c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436957} {:answer/id #uuid "861930b1-88a9-5573-b1ac-9183d339e59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436958} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbfaed1-b369-5612-a2fa-36c8475ceb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436959} {:answer/id #uuid "f9984cb1-95b5-56e2-9b37-0c405c4cefee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436960} {:answer/id #uuid "fad88501-95d6-5d04-967e-1baa7d034ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436961} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca34d82-cd28-5172-85b5-f4d4887562de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47e69096-fce9-5678-91d0-dfdd65324d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36908" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ -3 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fec85443-07a4-5564-aa88-c4b5100cb9d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436965}}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436956}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484343} :activity/id #uuid "a8c2fb89-d42b-514b-82ae-19ce4b7382f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df3b7c7-8ab5-56e8-8b9c-3e3f6b261779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404088} {:answer/id #uuid "bb986d69-47f8-539e-8e78-570956df6719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404089} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7bfd3a-84c3-5bd7-86de-43e46a13b773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404090} {:answer/id #uuid "72889b64-0d8f-5cf5-a078-304cd0270229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404091} {:answer/id #uuid "d85b9e14-5285-547f-8225-bac68ffb2c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404092} :answer-field/id #uuid "9085b031-ef66-5fae-9905-de9a5b2e5bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e995591-79aa-5800-bd07-e99e51f5d9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80373" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "0941e58b-0bd2-5235-9587-a3343780969f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efeb8db-be18-53ad-9f41-5d9c7901a0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269528} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec1cf70-97c1-5cb0-8bd5-06b5006830a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270376} {:answer/id #uuid "892aa3f2-2bcf-5365-9936-d7600a913769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270453} {:answer/id #uuid "b318af36-27af-5e2d-86e0-a473a223bf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270866} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd567a3-4432-56c1-9cad-efedcb5d9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270453} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f85dded-035f-5cb9-a048-9d2ff9e5113a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f80fc9d4-c782-54c4-af07-b3eb33afd65c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140038" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5513cc34-2b32-5506-9702-6da092a7c566"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3d8a9f-0837-5444-96f4-3343addf731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321730} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb2d859-49de-56b7-a465-a7874068e2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321894} {:answer/id #uuid "7867aeed-68dd-56a2-a34d-5ec26ea35c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d96ee6e-162e-5c5e-a698-98e97a131b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322387} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb16a4c-567b-5f54-9449-b0670f93ce27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321894} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cf44af-77c6-56e5-9933-c6fb76ed79fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a91b641-268c-57ee-aae6-245277497fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37600" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6191fc0-4b6b-54d3-87b6-db357e39ec0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0eb10f-8358-5f70-9dcf-5a12623a7ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404095} {:answer/id #uuid "1df643a8-80e1-5748-9179-fabd113dde47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404096} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd75f60-e366-51aa-9704-98e0a480bbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404097} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404098} {:answer/id #uuid "f62ab074-e099-5956-af5c-d32875cc38df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404098} :answer-field/id #uuid "76990587-1106-5838-a7d3-5abb529f309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0fb53181-29d9-5f89-8598-3c491720e6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80389" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "be260382-f713-59c8-aa98-36b16550df21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acfb64f-d399-5128-abd9-0fd718785c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321233} {:answer/id #uuid "32ca874e-c218-5348-8254-ebdd24f6b5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321535} {:answer/id #uuid "3d45e436-1b6c-5c26-990c-2a9bafba337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=15,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321624} {:answer/id #uuid "824319fd-3efa-515c-be89-065ca8419d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322173} {:answer/id #uuid "f428ad45-b7f1-5420-a618-a7f180e0107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322173} :answer-field/id #uuid "c580f2eb-b112-550e-99c4-120b3d89a440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "52d32399-93d9-5ce5-a996-5d60c1e0900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37610" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=15.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "e96641df-6f79-5c48-8011-c2641ddec815" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484302}}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483562}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499618} :activity/id #uuid "6b84de71-8564-517b-bc1e-eaec4c35e318" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08231533-7a2b-53e3-bb3b-f126a0945f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321209} {:answer/id #uuid "0b24974a-4a95-5964-a796-dfc9de32a894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321240} {:answer/id #uuid "3092878c-f858-5dc5-bfd5-49f9775ffbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321519} {:answer/id #uuid "81980f53-11b8-52ef-bde5-882f1c8ff79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322168} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e1dfdd-2680-5cf5-8e11-29ac4f3cc003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321240} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a63c75-dcf0-5bbc-84eb-ef1e705e4ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e30ae3bf-a95b-5038-8bdd-b265809e9d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-37629" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bea5b6f-6c05-5fe2-ad23-ee7c6d0550fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188ff12c-cc1a-5287-b72f-b735762cd4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345568} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4889c1-8a99-5c50-8d21-7e1f3fa8f0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346569} {:answer/id #uuid "97a5840f-59d5-5c8b-8a78-3953a0ad08a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346680} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bae287-d269-54df-b55a-ae11d68c915d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346833} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d5cbcb-c866-50ca-b5fd-06a7526077af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345568} :answer-field/id #uuid "00112189-18a5-5932-8481-85e29798e6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fad8010-b1d7-51d3-ae7c-0e9c4f636170" :question/math-academy-id "q-80453" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."} :step/id #uuid "13e8d76b-e707-545b-91b0-3646c86cd6fd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218d2232-b6b4-5353-9b44-bc2b615b173f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} {:answer/id #uuid "54242d0d-9f70-50fb-a1c7-1d7500836483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346065} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d2abcd-e82b-5bca-8c24-21c2c07d708d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6178168-bb39-5a85-9081-d612835e2505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346802} {:answer/id #uuid "ff40704f-1d0b-5ea5-8bf6-a3b2c43d0da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e355db22-060d-55f4-890a-5652cc9577f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "def1c542-80f5-5b03-88dc-ad7a755f7e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80371" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $K$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "66d23587-6ec7-592c-a133-1458d9c3ea42" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad559-0eae-5f15-bdf7-739e613b69ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404103} {:answer/id #uuid "d0038efe-a96a-5281-ad36-c4af7f9c03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} {:answer/id #uuid "c0406221-3155-5859-8236-e58b9f481700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404105} {:answer/id #uuid "d939f8e6-1f46-548c-8adc-229c62fd7d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} :answer-field/id #uuid "b352245b-eaa4-5fde-aea9-5afac2d00b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "6bf05bcb-22b4-547e-acbe-f43cad6ac0ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d029107-e148-55f6-a354-f22baa574250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321621} {:answer/id #uuid "56d1c400-959f-56c2-965a-3832f8ef8340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321833} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf1e1f5-79f6-557b-a763-a51889feac40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} {:answer/id #uuid "c23b64d9-c049-5e10-af78-713101e3ae50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} {:answer/id #uuid "db767106-a9b0-5dd4-8b49-d91b65e0543f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322654} :answer-field/id #uuid "79947e83-1100-52b3-a7fb-76ec24310ce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "334a7a7c-883e-54c4-9c3a-6e918b1922ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-37107" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c44bbf6a-6f50-5fd9-883e-0b5cb944bf04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499572}}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498723}]}
